  <p>This is a plain-language guide to what as-builts are in the context of an ADU, why you need them, what the drawings have to show, and how a 3D scan turns a multi-day measuring headache into a half-day site visit.</p>

  <h2>Why an ADU needs as-builts at all</h2>

  <p>An ADU is almost never a blank-slate project. It attaches to, converts, or sits beside a house and a lot that already exist, and the city needs to see how the new unit relates to those existing conditions. You cannot design a code-compliant ADU, or get it permitted, without an accurate picture of what's already on the property.</p>

  <p>For a garage or interior conversion, that means accurate existing floor plans, wall locations, ceiling heights, and window and door openings, so the designer knows what they're working with before adding a kitchen, a bathroom, and egress. For a detached ADU, it means an accurate site plan showing the main house, property lines, setbacks, and existing structures, so the new unit can be placed within the required distances.</p>

  <p>Original drawings, if the house even has them, are usually decades out of date, missing, or wrong after years of changes. The building department wants current, measured conditions, not what the house looked like in 1965.</p>



